## 4. Blueprint changes

The blueprint now recognizes a first-class Runtime Memory Module as the Runtime Dev substrate family for:

```text
working state
canonical memory
knowledge records
retrieval
storage projections
checkpoints
memory promotion
hydration
context-pack inputs
inspection surfaces
```

The selected long-term memory/state composition is documented as:

```text
SQLite WAL       local working continuity, task/session state, scratch/checkpoint state, promotion candidates
PostgreSQL      canonical memory and knowledge source of truth
Qdrant          rebuildable vector recall projection over canonical/promoted memory IDs
JanusGraph      rebuildable relationship projection for structural traversal
```

The blueprint states the core memory invariant:

```text
SQLite WAL operates.
PostgreSQL remembers.
Qdrant recalls.
JanusGraph relates.
Reranker prioritizes.
Context packer feeds.
```

It also states that PostgreSQL owns canonical truth; SQLite owns local working continuity; Qdrant and JanusGraph are rebuildable projections; and vector/graph projection results must hydrate through PostgreSQL before becoming trusted model context.

---

## 5. Memory promotion and retrieval guardrails

The blueprint now documents the critical memory flow:

```text
1. Runtime activity writes local working state to SQLite WAL.
2. Scratch/checkpoint state may become a promotion candidate.
3. Promotion guards validate scope, source, confidence, lifecycle, and promotion reason.
4. Approved records write to PostgreSQL canonical memory/knowledge.
5. PostgreSQL emits memory_events/outbox records.
6. Projection workers update Qdrant and JanusGraph.
7. Retrieval from Qdrant/JanusGraph returns IDs/candidates only.
8. PostgreSQL hydrates and validates canonical records.
9. Reranker ranks useful candidates.
10. Context packer builds bounded model input.
```

Hard rules added to the blueprint:

```text
No vector or graph projection is canonical truth.
No scratch state becomes long-term memory without promotion.
No stale/superseded/expired memory enters critical context without explicit override.
Qdrant and JanusGraph must not directly feed trusted context without PostgreSQL hydration.
The reranker decides relevance and priority, not truth.
Capability services and backend adapters do not interpret cognitive meaning.
```

The wording also clarifies that the Runtime Memory Module owns storage-level promotion mechanics, while a future Cognitive Graph Runtime may request or decide control-layer promotion only through Runtime Memory Module promotion surfaces and guards.

